= Microwave Waveguides and Resonators
<subsec:waveguide>
Waveguides and resonators are key elements of microwave systems, which define the geometric modes of the electromagnetic fields. The modal properties of microwave propagation can be described completely by the electric field profile satisfying @bondeson_computational_2005

$ nabla times \( mu_r^(- 1) nabla times upright(bold(E)) \) - omega^2 / c^2 (epsilon_r - frac(i sigma, omega epsilon_0)) upright(bold(E)) = 0 $<eq:emsolver>

at all points. Here, $mu_r$ is the relative permeability, $epsilon_r$ and $epsilon_0$ are the relative and vacuum permittivity respectively, $sigma$ is the material conductivity, $c$ is the speed of light in vacuum, and $omega$ is the microwave frequency. In arbitrary structures, the modal properties can be found by applying equation (#ref(<eq:emsolver>, supplement: none)) through an FEM.

Waveguides are a useful structure with analytic solutions to their natural mode sets @pozar_microwave_2012. Commonly, they are rectangular or cylindrical metal tubes which provide boundary conditions in two transverse directions (x and y) through the metal walls, while leaving the longitudinal (z) direction unconstrained. This separation of transverse and longitudinal conditions reduces to a paraxial system, where a set of transverse modes satisfy equation (#ref(<eq:emsolver>, supplement: none)) for the waveguide boundary conditions @jackson_classical_2009 independent of the longitudinal mode. This produces a set of transverse fields, shown in Fig. #ref(<fig:EMDevice>, supplement: none)#text("a")), which can propagate through the waveguide structure. For rectangular waveguides, these are separated into transverse electric (TE) and transverse magnetic (TM), with TEml denoting the TE mode of orders m,l. Any combination of these modes can, in principle, be selectively excited to form deterministic intensity patterns within the waveguides and engineer the interactions with resonators or other devices. Modern technologies struggle with selective excitation of higher-order waveguide modes ($m \, l gt.eq 2$), but research is ongoing to improve this functionality with prospects in phase-selective devices @lauterio-cruz_higher_2023@ruskuc_excitation_2018 and microwave analogs to spatial light modulation using controllable metasurfaces @wu_intelligent_2019@cui_coding_2014. Unless otherwise specified, all waveguides in this work are copper WR-90 (0.4in x 0.9in cross section) to use a commercially available product compatible with the selected frequency range of study.

Resonators, particularly low-finesse Fabry-Perot resonators @jackson_classical_2009, form standing waves by applying weak boundary conditions on the longitudinal mode. A Fabry-Perot resonator, shown in Fig. #ref(<fig:EMDevice>, supplement: none)#text("b")), consists of two partially reflective, inwards facing mirrors with a gap (cavity) between them @hecht_optics_2017@pozar_microwave_2012. The one simple mirror commonly used in resonators is a Distributed Bragg Reflector (DBR) mirror @saleh_fundamentals_2001. These consist of layers of alternating dispersive materials with $lambda \/ 4$ thickness, for $lambda$ being the wavelength in the medium, where interference effects between the layers enhance reflection. Aperture mirrors are also commonly integrated with waveguide technologies @pozar_microwave_2012, but they are not discussed further in this work. Using resonators in waveguides permits enhancement of internal fields. In cavities, which satisfy the round-trip phase condition $phi.alt_(r t) = beta L = 2 pi n$ for propagation constant $beta$, cavity length $L$, and integer $n$ for all points on a wavefront, the counter-propagating traveling waves form internal standing waves, and thus increase all light-matter interactions at antinodes of the modal patterns. The amount of internal field enhancement can be quantified by the Q-factor $Q = frac(omega, Delta omega)$ @hecht_optics_2017, a metric of energy stored in the resonator mode. This relationship highlights the largest limitation for implementing resonator systems; the inverse proportionality with the linewidth $Delta omega$ dictates the spread of frequencies that can form quasi-resonant cavity modes. In simple terms, the larger the internal field enhancement, the smaller the range of frequencies the resonator will accept, reducing tunability and increasing the importance of thermal/mechanical stability. The Q-factor is proportional to the reflectivity of the resonator mirrors, and so varying the number of DBR-layers -- where more layers lead to higher reflection -- allows direct control of the Q-factor. The one-dimensional calculated intensity pattern for the cavity in Fig. #ref(<fig:EMDevice>, supplement: none)#text("b")) is given in Fig. #ref(<fig:EMDevice>, supplement: none)#text("c")). Recent work has demonstrated effective methods of engineering the standing wave modes for optical frequencies @ossiander_metasurface-stabilized_2023, and similar methods in the microwave regime provide control over the intracavity intensity patterns.

