= Microwave Heating and Current Induction
<subsec:heat>
The majority of microwave heating occurs due to two distinct mechanisms: dielectric heating and ohmic heating. Dielectric heating occurs when the oscillating electric field drives molecular polarization out of phase @metaxas_industrial_2008@sun_review_2016. These losses cause dipolar rotation and drive the movement of charges, causing various forms of resistive heating in polar molecules, disordered lattices, and powders. The heat generation per unit volume can be given by @sun_review_2016

$ (frac(d P, d V))_(d i) = omega / 2 epsilon_0 epsilon'' \| upright(bold(E)) \|^2 $<eq:dielectric_heat> where $epsilon''$ is the imaginary component of permittivity $epsilon_r = epsilon' - j epsilon''$. Generally, $epsilon''$ is small for non-polar, ordered lattices with values as low as $upright(t a n) delta approx 2.7 upright(x) 10^(- 5)$ for alumina @alford_sintered_1996. This loss can become significant for polar molecules, such as epoxy with $upright(t a n) delta = 0.52$ @calisir_designing_2025, and can increase due to Maxwell-Wagner polarization @metaxas_industrial_2008 in the presence of defects and material interfaces, leading to a sizable difference between silica quartz ($upright(t a n) delta approx 2 upright(x) 10^(- 4)$) and fused silica ($upright(t a n) delta approx 10^(- 3)$) @zhu_investigation_2009@lide_physical_2005. Importantly, Maxwell-Wagner polarization suggests that the absorption of bulk dielectric might be an underestimation of the absorption of a powdered dielectric due to the concentration of material surfaces.

In ohmic heating, in contrast, the oscillating field drives currents through conductive materials and energy is dissipated by resistive heating @sun_review_2016. In this regime,

$ (frac(d P, d V))_(o h m) = sigma_(e f f) / 2 \| upright(bold(E)) \|^2 $<eq:ohmic_heat> where $sigma_(e f f)$ is the effective conductivity of a conductor or composite mixture. This heating is particularly relevant to metals, where the skin depth $delta = sqrt(frac(2, mu sigma omega))$ determines the heating volume @jackson_classical_2009. Both dipolar heating and ohmic heating are natively captured by the loss tangent @pozar_microwave_2012

$ upright(t a n) delta = epsilon'' / epsilon' + frac(sigma, omega epsilon_0 epsilon') $<eq:loss_tangent> which fully quantifies electric field absorption as $epsilon_r = epsilon' \( 1 - j upright(t a n) delta \)$.

With heating power and location determined by the field profile and material properties, the total heating profile can be expressed by the heat conduction equation @incropera_principles_2013

$ rho C_p frac(partial T, partial t) = nabla dot.op \( k nabla T \) + P_(i n) $<eq:heatcond> where $rho$ is the mass density, $C_p$ is the specific heat capacity, $k$ is the thermal conductivity, and $P_(i n)$ is the total heating power.

= Material Properties and Composite Design
<subsec:mats>
The electromagnetic modes and thermal profiles central to this work depend on the material properties of the feedstock. As such, a physical model of microwave heating requires accurate modeling of material properties. Powder melting-based technologies require careful consideration to electromagnetic properties, thermal properties, and shock resistance of all materials, with several properties being evaluated in bulk and several specific to the particles @gibbons_metal_2024. The shock resistance and melting temperatures can be considered as a threshold value for each material species separately. Due to practical limitations on FEM mesh sizes, however, properties relevant to electromagnetic mode formation and heat transfer must be treated as a bulk properties over a larger volume.

All powder feedstocks are necessarily composites due to imperfect packing factors ($< 1$), and thus require effective mean approximations to find the equivalent bulk properties of the powder volume @gutierrez_electromagnetic_2019. These approximations are valid when the radiation wavelength is much larger than the average powder grain length ($lambda gt.double d$), as scattering becomes negligible in this regime @hecht_optics_2017. In these cases, the effective mean approximation can determine the effective properties of a homogeneous powder with a vacuum medium (or inert gas) by its packing fraction or weight of the contributions of multiple powder materials in a mixture. Notably, mixing powders provides a valuable pathway to engineering the electromagnetic and thermal properties of the bulk medium, which can improve compatibility with VAM processes. In this work, both single powder feedstock and mixtures are considered. A graphical representation of the true powder material and effective bulk medium are represented in Fig. #ref(<fig:Brug>, supplement: none)#text("a")) and Fig. #ref(<fig:Brug>, supplement: none)#text("b")) respectively.

For a mixture of densely-packed dielectric powders we consider effective electromagnetic properties from the Bruggeman Effective Medium Approximation @gutierrez_electromagnetic_2019@stroud_effective_1998 where the properties of medium 1 and medium 2 reduce to a single effective value where

$ f frac(x_1 - x_(e f f), x_1 + 2 x_(e f f)) + \( 1 - f \) frac(x_2 - x_(e f f), x_2 + 2 x_(e f f)) = 0 $<eq:brug> for volume fraction $f$ and $x$ is any of $epsilon$, $mu$, or $sigma$. Importantly, this approximation is defined for complex values and thus naturally modifies the absorption term $epsilon''$ in addition to the dispersive term. This model fails, however, for dielectric-metal mixtures past the percolation threshold $f_c approx 0.2$. In this regime, the metal grains begin to contact in extended chains and the electric absorption rises steeply. Here, the permittivity is found by @bergman_critical_1977 $ epsilon_(e f f) = epsilon_(b r u g) + frac(i sigma_(e f f), epsilon_0 omega) = epsilon'_(e f f) \( 1 - j upright(t a n) delta_(e f f) \) $<eq:perc_ep> for effective permittivity $epsilon_(b r u g)$ from equation (#ref(<eq:brug>, supplement: none)), field angular frequency $omega$, and effective conductivity @bergman_critical_1977

$ sigma_(e f f) = sigma_m \( f - f_c \)^t . $<eq:perc_con> Here, $sigma_m$ is the conductivity of the bulk metal and $t$ is the critical exponent which is typically assumed to be 2 for random powders. The effective thermal properties $rho$, $C_p$, and $k$ can be approximated similarly using a modified Bruggeman Effective Mean Approximation for random, compacted powders @ordonez-miranda_generalized_2010.

This approach provides tunability of material properties, and thus allows access to regimes of higher conductivity for similar materials. A notable tradeoff for dielectric materials is the absorption and the natural reflection. Moderately conductive materials have a naturally high-absorption in small particle mixtures @bohren_absorption_1998, and thus mixing lower-loss dielectric powders with moderate conductors allows enhanced absorption. This is detailed in Fig. 2b) of the main text using several ceramics and polymers common in microwave systems, and compares their absorptive ($upright(t a n) delta \)$ and reflective ($\| epsilon_r \|$) properties to that of a fused-silica carbon black powder mixture. This demonstrates the value of composite powders in tuning the absorptivity to regions uncommon for non-polar dielectric materials. The alternative to poor conductors, such as carbon black, would be the addition of polar materials which would increase the absorptivity but possibly lead to unstable material properties near resonances@jonscher_dielectric_1999.

For this approach, any low-loss dielectric can be used, but the required power will be inversely proportional with the loss tangent of the material. Using these composites lowers the power requirements, and offers a pathway to designing composite solids with favourable thermomechanical.

