= Temperature Dependence of Material Properties
<subsec:temp>
Electrical and thermal material properties have a strong dependence on temperature, often with notably non-linearity around phase changes @yamaguchi_assignment_2011@garcia_temperature-dependent_1998. Thermal properties of bulk ceramics and metals are often well documented, and the thermal sensitivity is included through interpolation of detailed property tables. Electromagnetic properties, however, have less reliable documentation for elevated temperatures and simplified models must be used to predict $epsilon'$, $upright(t a n) delta$, and $sigma$.

In non-polar materials, $epsilon'$ experiences only a mild temperature dependence @passerat_de_silans_temperature_2009 and so we model the relationship as a linear extrapolation

$ epsilon' \( T \) = epsilon' \( T_0 \) + alpha \( T - T_0 \) $<eq:perm_temp> where $alpha$ is an experimental proportionality constant and the reference temperature $T_0$ is taken to be room temperature. This linearization is usually valid for a small, experimentally verified temperature range and does not account for nonlinearities induced by phase changes. While useful in probing system sensitivities, this approximation is likely the largest source of potential non-physicality in the results. Access to detailed material data would greatly improve the reliability of the simulations, and allow direct mapping to experimental systems. Alternatively, improved theoretical models, as has been developed for materials with well defined band gaps as a function of temperature @ren_novel_2025, would also strengthen the results.

The loss tangent of temperature dependence commonly uses an activation energy model where @jonscher_dielectric_1999 $ upright(t a n) delta \( T \) = upright(t a n) delta_0 thin e^(- frac(E_a, k_B T)) $<eq:loss_temp> for a high-temperature loss tangent $upright(t a n) delta_0$, Boltzmann constant $k_B$, and activation energy $E_a$. The activation energy is an experimentally characterized property, known for various dielectrics including alumina @vila_dielectric_1998 and silica @kakiuchida_dielectric_1999. The activation energy is the energy threshold required for lattice vibrations or defect-densities to induce absorption @nowick_anelastic_1972. Polar materials can be accurately characterized similarly using a Debye relaxation model @jonscher_new_1981.

Electrical conductivity follows the same exponential relationship, called the Arrhenius model @atkins_atkins_2023, which can be used for any conductive material. These simulations only investigate copper and tungsten metals, however, and so trusted empirical formula are used, giving @young_university_2012 $ sigma_c = frac(5.8 e^7, 1 + \( 0.0039 K^(- 1) \) \( T - T_0 \)) thin S \/ m $<eq:cond_cu> for copper and @young_university_2012 $ sigma_W = frac(1.8 e^7, 1 + \( 0.0045 K^(- 1) \) \( T - T_0 \)) thin S \/ m $<eq:cond_w> for tungsten with reference temperature $T_0 = 293 K$ ($0^compose C$).

= Model Validation
<subsec:validation>
Convergence tests were used as validation of the internal consistency of the model. The presumed maximum mesh size of $lambda \/ 10$ was evaluated in the linear resonator design. The maximum temperature was evaluated after the last time-interval as a function of maximum mesh dimension for small timestep $t = 0.1$ms. The results are graphed in Fig. #ref(<fig:convergence>, supplement: none). This physical convergence is complemented by a numerical convergence predicted by the reciprocal step size decrease. Further simulations were validated by the internal COMSOL convergence plots.

