= An Angled Interface for Field Localization in Rectangular Retro-reflectors
<an-angled-interface-for-field-localization-in-rectangular-retro-reflectors>
In the external printer design, an angled surface between the air/vacuum region and the silica region within the waveguide boundaries, shown in Fig. #ref(<fig:angsurf>, supplement: none)#text("a")), breaks the paraxial symmetry and induces transverse mode mixing @hill_electromagnetic_1998. These surfaces can be used to form more uniform heating distributions and, with impedance matching, increase the energy density in the silica, reducing the power requirements for efficient heating.

We evaluated the energy density for several interface angles and input frequencies. The results, shown in Fig. #ref(<fig:angsurf>, supplement: none)#text("b")), highlight the increase in field concentration in the silica at the design frequency of 10 GHz, and the erratic frequency dependence of angled surfaces due to impedance matching with scattered modes. Using an angle of $55^compose$, the power requirements are 8.3 times lower to achieve the same heating rates as with a normal interface, demonstrating one simple change which could greatly improve the experimental feasibility of the device. However, caution must be exercised when designing systems with angled reflecting surfaces. The corners of the angled surfaces can lead to mesh singularities with improper refinement, limiting physicality of the results, and the mode mixing can excite modes which concentrate fields inside the silica, heating the dielectric rather than the metal boundaries.

